    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The Gargano Promontory, located on the Adriatic coast of southern Italy, has been highly deformed and uplifted more than 100 m above the adjacent Apulian foreland. Our previous detailed field mapping has defined two main fault systems in the region which controlled the uplift of the Gargano Block. The first system is oriented Nw-Se, shows primarily reverse to oblique-reverse displacements,  and is pervasive throughout the Gargano. The second sytem strikes e-W, bounds the northern and southern margins of the uplifted block, and extends east into the offshore Adriatic. The Structural data collected indicate that the two systems have interacted to produce the uplift of the region. The geometry of the E-W fault system defines a right step in the left-lateral fault system, resulting in a  zone of compression within the step. Uplift was accommodated by reverse motion on the NW-SE fault system as a result of increased compressional stress within the stepover region.\r\n\r\nNumerical models incorporating the observed boundary fault geometries and their slip sense support this interpretation. Several cases were examined, varying both the remote stress orientations and fault friction. Model results agree closely with field observations, and allow us to constrain the paleostress orientations and frictional characteristics of the boundary faults at the time of the deformation. Increased compressive stresses occur within the fault segment overstep region, and computed stress trajectories agree closely with the compressional features observed in the field. This mechanism of uplift has not been recognized previously in the foreland of the southern Apennines, but is observed in the complexly deformed interior of the Apennine fold-thrust belt, both at the surface and at depth.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
